    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they are asked to do two incompatible jobs at once. They are supposed to be a strategic instrument, a way of saying what matters and what doesn't, and they are also supposed to be a promise, a schedule of things that will exist by certain dates. Strategy requires the freedom to change your mind when the world tells you something new. Promises require that you don't. Put both demands on a single document and it will quietly become a promise, because promises are what other people remember.\n\nThe first failure, then, is that roadmaps get treated as commitments by everyone except the people who wrote them. A sales leader sees a feature in the Q3 column and mentions it to a prospect. A support director tells an angry customer relief is coming in the spring. Marketing starts drafting the launch. By the time the product team learns something that should change the plan, the plan has been socialized into an obligation. Changing it now looks like failure rather than learning, so the team ships the thing anyway, late and diminished, and calls it a win.\n\nThe second failure is that roadmaps are usually lists of outputs rather than statements of intent. A row that says \"notifications revamp\" tells you nothing about what problem is being solved or how you would know if it worked. Teams building against output lists optimize for shipping, because shipping is the only thing being measured. You end up with a year of completed items and no discernible improvement in the business, which is how organizations arrive at the strange conclusion that they are executing well and losing anyway.\n\nThird, roadmaps are built on estimates that everyone knows are fiction and no one is allowed to say so. Effort guesses made before discovery are wrong by large multiples, and they are wrong in one direction. So the calendar is fantasy from the first day, and every subsequent week is spent negotiating which parts of the fantasy to abandon. This is exhausting and corrosive, and it teaches engineers that planning is theater.\n\nFourth, most roadmaps are aggregations rather than choices. Each stakeholder gets something, so nothing gets enough. The document becomes a peace treaty among departments, which is a perfectly reasonable political achievement and a terrible allocation of attention. Real roadmaps say no loudly, and saying no loudly is expensive for the person holding the pen, which is why it so rarely happens.\n\nWhat tends to work better is modesty about time and clarity about purpose. Be specific about what you are doing now, name the problem you expect to solve next and why it matters, and keep the far future deliberately vague, described in terms of the outcomes you are chasing rather than the features you imagine will produce them. Revisit it often enough that changing it feels normal. Tell the rest of the company how to read it, including which parts are firm and which are hypotheses.\n\nNone of this makes planning easy. It just stops the plan from lying.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they treat the future as something we can schedule. Teams spend weeks lining up features and dates as if they already know what customers will need six months from now and how long every piece of work will take. Then the world moves. A new competitor appears, a key assumption turns out to be wrong, or someone important leaves the company. The carefully drawn plan starts looking like fiction.\n\nPeople also turn the roadmap into a commitment instead of a working hypothesis. Sales uses the dates to close deals. Leadership uses the document to judge whether the team is delivering. When the inevitable changes happen, trust disappears. The thing that was supposed to keep everyone aligned becomes the thing everyone argues about.\n\nA lot of roadmaps describe solutions rather than the problems worth solving. They become a list of screens and capabilities decided last quarter. Fresh evidence shows up, but the team keeps executing the old list because changing the roadmap feels like admitting failure. The document stops helping people think and starts telling them what to build no matter what they learn.\n\nWe consistently underestimate complexity. Work takes longer than the slides suggested. Dependencies surface late. Maintenance and technical debt that were never on the roadmap suddenly demand attention. Discovery that would reduce risk gets skipped because it does not look like progress against the published plan.\n\nCommunication gaps finish the job. The group that created the roadmap rarely stays in close conversation with the people building the product or talking to customers every day. Hidden assumptions stay hidden until they blow up. Trade-offs that should have been explicit stay implicit until it is too late.\n\nThe roadmaps that survive are the ones treated as living sketches rather than finished paintings. They stay detailed only for the near term and stay deliberately vague further out. They get revised often. They focus more on the outcomes the business needs than on the exact features that might produce those outcomes. Most companies do not operate that way. They produce a clean looking artifact, present it, and then wonder why it did not last. Planning gives the comforting feeling of control. That feeling is usually an illusion, and that is why most product roadmaps fail.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
